## Overview

Create a separate timer wheel implementation for Lab runtime that operates on virtual time.

## Requirements

### Virtual Time Wheel
```rust
pub struct VirtualTimerWheel {
    /// Current virtual time (ticks, not wall-clock).
    current_tick: u64,

    /// Wheel structure (same as real timer wheel).
    wheel: HierarchicalTimerWheel,

    /// Timers awaiting insertion (for deterministic ordering).
    pending: Vec<(u64, TimerNode)>,
}

impl VirtualTimerWheel {
    /// Advance virtual time to the next timer deadline.
    /// Returns list of expired timers in deterministic order.
    pub fn advance_to_next(&mut self) -> Vec<Waker> {
        let next = self.next_deadline()?;
        self.advance_to(next)
    }

    /// Advance virtual time by a specific amount.
    pub fn advance_by(&mut self, ticks: u64) -> Vec<Waker> {
        self.advance_to(self.current_tick + ticks)
    }

    /// Advance to specific tick, processing all timers up to that point.
    pub fn advance_to(&mut self, target_tick: u64) -> Vec<Waker> {
        let mut expired = Vec::new();
        while self.current_tick < target_tick {
            // Process next wheel slot
            expired.extend(self.wheel.tick());
            self.current_tick += 1;
        }

        // Sort expired by timer ID for determinism
        expired.sort_by_key(|w| w.timer_id);
        expired
    }
}
```

### Lab Runtime Integration
```rust
impl LabRuntime {
    /// Advance virtual time until a task can make progress.
    fn advance_time_to_next_event(&mut self) {
        if let Some(deadline) = self.timer_wheel.next_deadline() {
            let expired = self.timer_wheel.advance_to(deadline);
            for waker in expired {
                waker.wake();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Determinism Guarantees
- Same tick → same timers expire
- Expiration order is deterministic (sorted by timer ID)
- No wall-clock dependencies

