# Bug Fix: Mutex Waiter Queue Bounded Growth (COMPLETED)

## Status: FIXED in session 2026-01-31

## Location
`src/sync/mutex.rs:244-272` - LockFuture::poll

## Problem
Each poll of a waiting future could add a new entry to the waiter queue, causing
unbounded memory growth if a future was polled repeatedly without being woken.

## Original Issue
```rust
fn poll(...) {
    // ...
    // Every poll added a new waiter!
    state.waiters.push_back(Waiter {
        waker: context.waker().clone(),
        queued: Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(true)),
    });
}
```

## Fix Applied
Track waiter registration with per-future state:

```rust
pub struct LockFuture<'a, 'b, T> {
    mutex: &'a Mutex<T>,
    cx: &'b Cx,
    waiter: Option<Arc<AtomicBool>>,  // Track if already registered
}

fn poll(...) {
    let mut new_waiter = None;
    match self.waiter.as_ref() {
        Some(waiter) if !waiter.load(Ordering::Acquire) => {
            // Re-register (was dequeued but not woken properly)
            waiter.store(true, Ordering::Release);
            state.waiters.push_back(Waiter {
                waker: context.waker().clone(),
                queued: Arc::clone(waiter),
            });
        }
        Some(_) => {}  // Already registered, don't add again
        None => {
            // First registration
            let waiter = Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(true));
            state.waiters.push_back(Waiter {
                waker: context.waker().clone(),
                queued: Arc::clone(&waiter),
            });
            new_waiter = Some(waiter);
        }
    }
    // ...
    if let Some(waiter) = new_waiter {
        self.waiter = Some(waiter);
    }
}
```

