# Race Combinator with Loser Draining

## Purpose
race(f1, f2) runs two futures concurrently and returns when the FIRST completes. The loser is cancelled AND DRAINED. This is the alternative composition operator (⊕) from the near-semiring.

## Critical Invariant: LOSERS ARE DRAINED

**This is non-negotiable (I5)**. Unlike other runtimes that abandon losers:

```rust
// BAD (tokio-style): Loser is dropped, may leak resources
select! {
    r1 = f1 => r1,
    r2 = f2 => r2,  // f2 dropped if f1 wins, may hold locks!
}

// GOOD (Asupersync): Loser is cancelled AND awaited
race(f1, f2)  // Winner returns, loser cancelled, THEN loser awaited to completion
```

## Semantics

```
race(f1, f2):
  t1 ← spawn(f1)
  t2 ← spawn(f2)
  (winner, loser) ← select_first_complete(t1, t2)
  cancel(loser)
  await(loser)  // CRITICAL: drain the loser
  return winner.outcome
```

## Implementation

```rust
pub async fn race<F1, F2, T>(
    scope: &Scope<'_>,
    f1: F1,
    f2: F2,
) -> Outcome<T>
where
    F1: Future<Output = T>,
    F2: Future<Output = T>,
{
    // Create a subregion for the race
    scope.region(|sub| async {
        let h1 = sub.spawn(f1);
        let h2 = sub.spawn(f2);

        // Wait for first to complete
        let (winner_outcome, loser_handle) = select_first(h1, h2).await;

        // Cancel the loser
        loser_handle.cancel(CancelReason::race_loser());

        // CRITICAL: Wait for loser to drain
        let _ = loser_handle.join().await;

        winner_outcome
    }).await
}
```

## select_first Implementation

```rust
async fn select_first<T>(
    h1: JoinHandle<'_, T>,
    h2: JoinHandle<'_, T>,
) -> (Outcome<T>, JoinHandle<'_, T>) {
    // Poll both, return first to complete
    poll_fn(|cx| {
        // Check h1
        if let Poll::Ready(o) = h1.poll_join(cx) {
            return Poll::Ready((o, h2));
        }
        // Check h2
        if let Poll::Ready(o) = h2.poll_join(cx) {
            return Poll::Ready((o, h1));
        }
        Poll::Pending
    }).await
}
```

## Why Draining Matters

Consider a race where the loser holds a lock:

```rust
let result = race(
    async {
        let _guard = mutex.lock().await;  // Holds lock
        slow_operation().await
    },
    async {
        fast_operation().await
    },
).await;

// If we don't drain the loser:
// - Lock is never released!
// - Other tasks waiting on mutex deadlock

// With draining:
// - Loser receives cancel
// - Loser's drop runs, releasing lock
// - System is consistent
```

## Algebraic Laws

### Associativity
```
race(race(a, b), c) ≃ race(a, race(b, c))
```

### Commutativity (schedule-dependent)
```
race(a, b) ≃ race(b, a)  // Same winner set, different selection
```

### Identity
```
race(a, never) ≃ a  // never = future that never completes
```

### Distributivity with Join (for deduplication)
```
race(join(a, b), join(a, c)) ≃ join(a, race(b, c))
// Don't run 'a' twice!
```

## race_all

Generalized to N futures:

```rust
pub async fn race_all<I, F, T>(
    scope: &Scope<'_>,
    futures: I,
) -> Outcome<T>
where
    I: IntoIterator<Item = F>,
    F: Future<Output = T>,
{
    scope.region(|sub| async {
        let handles: Vec<_> = futures
            .into_iter()
            .map(|f| sub.spawn(f))
            .collect();

        // Wait for first
        let (winner_idx, winner_outcome) = select_first_of_many(&handles).await;

        // Cancel and drain all losers
        for (i, h) in handles.into_iter().enumerate() {
            if i != winner_idx {
                h.cancel(CancelReason::race_loser());
                let _ = h.join().await;  // Drain
            }
        }

        winner_outcome
    }).await
}
```

