## Overview

Implement a specialized builder for the Lab runtime with deterministic testing options.

## Background

Lab runtime has unique configuration needs: deterministic RNG, virtual time, scheduling oracles, fault injection. A dedicated builder makes these discoverable.

## Implementation

```rust
pub struct LabRuntimeBuilder {
    rng_seed: Option<u64>,
    scheduling_oracle: Option<Box<dyn SchedulingOracle>>,
    time_mode: TimeMode,
    fault_injection: Option<FaultConfig>,
    trace_recorder: Option<TraceRecorder>,
}

pub enum TimeMode {
    Virtual,          // Lab controls time
    RealWithScale(f64), // Real time, but scaled
}

impl LabRuntimeBuilder {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self::default()
    }

    /// Set the RNG seed for deterministic execution.
    /// Same seed = same scheduling decisions.
    pub fn rng_seed(mut self, seed: u64) -> Self {
        self.rng_seed = Some(seed);
        self
    }

    /// Use a custom scheduling oracle for controlled execution order.
    pub fn scheduling_oracle(mut self, oracle: impl SchedulingOracle + 'static) -> Self {
        self.scheduling_oracle = Some(Box::new(oracle));
        self
    }

    /// Enable virtual time (default for Lab).
    pub fn virtual_time(mut self) -> Self {
        self.time_mode = TimeMode::Virtual;
        self
    }

    /// Enable trace recording for replay debugging.
    pub fn record_trace(mut self) -> Self {
        self.trace_recorder = Some(TraceRecorder::new());
        self
    }

    /// Configure fault injection for chaos testing.
    pub fn fault_injection(mut self, config: FaultConfig) -> Self {
        self.fault_injection = Some(config);
        self
    }

    pub fn build(self) -> LabRuntime {
        LabRuntime::from_builder(self)
    }
}
```

