# Task: Rewrite TcpStream Read/Write with Reactor Wakeup

## What

Remove the Phase 0 busy-loop pattern from TcpStream's AsyncRead/AsyncWrite implementations and use proper reactor-based wakeup.

## Location

`src/net/tcp/stream.rs`

## Current Implementation (Phase 0)

```rust
impl AsyncRead for TcpStream {
    fn poll_read(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>, buf: &mut ReadBuf<'_>) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
        match (&*self.inner).read(buf.unfilled()) {
            Ok(n) => { buf.advance(n); Poll::Ready(Ok(())) }
            Err(ref e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::WouldBlock => {
                cx.waker().wake_by_ref();  // Phase 0: busy-loop!
                Poll::Pending
            }
            Err(e) => Poll::Ready(Err(e)),
        }
    }
}
```

## New Implementation (Phase 2)

```rust
pub struct TcpStream {
    inner: net::TcpStream,
    registration: Option<Registration>,
}

impl AsyncRead for TcpStream {
    fn poll_read(
        self: Pin<&mut Self>,
        cx: &mut Context<'_>,
        buf: &mut ReadBuf<'_>,
    ) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
        // Attempt the read
        match self.inner.read(buf.unfilled()) {
            Ok(0) => {
                // EOF
                Poll::Ready(Ok(()))
            }
            Ok(n) => {
                buf.advance(n);
                Poll::Ready(Ok(()))
            }
            Err(ref e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::WouldBlock => {
                // No data available right now
                // The waker is already registered with the reactor via Registration
                // Reactor will wake when data arrives
                //
                // Note: We could update the waker here if needed:
                // self.registration.as_ref().map(|r| r.update_waker(cx.waker().clone()));

                Poll::Pending
            }
            Err(e) => Poll::Ready(Err(e)),
        }
    }
}

impl AsyncWrite for TcpStream {
    fn poll_write(
        self: Pin<&mut Self>,
        cx: &mut Context<'_>,
        buf: &[u8],
    ) -> Poll<io::Result<usize>> {
        match self.inner.write(buf) {
            Ok(n) => Poll::Ready(Ok(n)),
            Err(ref e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::WouldBlock => {
                // Can't write right now, reactor will wake when writable
                Poll::Pending
            }
            Err(e) => Poll::Ready(Err(e)),
        }
    }

    fn poll_flush(self: Pin<&mut Self>, _cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
        // TCP flush is a no-op (data is sent immediately)
        Poll::Ready(Ok(()))
    }

    fn poll_shutdown(self: Pin<&mut Self>, _cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
        // Shutdown write half
        self.inner.shutdown(Shutdown::Write)?;
        Poll::Ready(Ok(()))
    }
}
```

## Waker Update Strategy

Two options for handling waker updates:

### Option A: Update on Every Poll (Simple)
```rust
// In poll_read
if let Some(reg) = &self.registration {
    reg.update_waker(cx.waker().clone());
}
```

### Option B: Update Only When Changed (Efficient)
```rust
// Store last waker, compare before update
if self.last_waker.as_ref().map_or(true, |w| !w.will_wake(cx.waker())) {
    self.registration.update_waker(cx.waker().clone());
    self.last_waker = Some(cx.waker().clone());
}
```

We'll start with Option A for simplicity, optimize later if needed.

## Registration Lifecycle

1. **Created**: During TcpStream::connect() or from_std()
2. **Used**: poll_read/poll_write return Pending
3. **Dropped**: When TcpStream is dropped (RAII cleanup)

## Edge-Triggered Considerations

With edge-triggered reactors (EPOLLET, kqueue default):
- Must read/write until WouldBlock to get next event
- If only partial read, must re-arm or remember state

Our implementation handles this:
- Return Pending on WouldBlock (user will poll again)
- Each poll attempt reads/writes what it can

